{
  "pyq_questions": [
    {
      "id": "q_jee_2024_ph_01",
      "examId": "jee_main",
      "exam": "JEE Main",
      "subjectId": "physics",
      "subject": "Physics",
      "chapterId": "rotational_motion",
      "chapter": "Rotational Motion",
      "topicId": "moment_of_inertia",
      "topic": "Moment of Inertia & Rolling Motion",
      "year": 2024,
      "shift": "29 Jan Shift 1",
      "type": "MCQ",
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "revisionZone": "high_yield",
      "ncertPages": "Class 11, pp. 164-172",
      "statementHtml": "<p>A solid sphere of mass $M$ and radius $R$ rolls without slipping down an inclined plane of inclination $\\theta$. The acceleration of the center of mass of the sphere is:</p>",
      "options": [
        { "key": "A", "html": "<p>$\\frac{5}{7} g \\sin \\theta$</p>" },
        { "key": "B", "html": "<p>$\\frac{2}{5} g \\sin \\theta$</p>" },
        { "key": "C", "html": "<p>$\\frac{1}{2} g \\sin \\theta$</p>" },
        { "key": "D", "html": "<p>$\\frac{7}{10} g \\sin \\theta$</p>" }
      ],
      "correctOption": "A",
      "numericalAnswer": null,
      "numericalTolerance": 0.0,
      "correctMarks": 4,
      "negativeMarks": -1,
      "solutionHtml": "<p>For a body rolling without slipping down an incline of angle $\\theta$, the linear acceleration is given by $$a = \\frac{g \\sin \\theta}{1 + \\frac{I}{M R^2}}$$ For a solid sphere, $I = \\frac{2}{5} M R^2$, so $a = \\frac{g \\sin \\theta}{1 + 2/5} = \\frac{5}{7} g \\sin \\theta$.</p>",
      "commonMistakeType": "formula_mixup",
      "hasVideoSolution": true,
      "videoContentId": "vdo_ph_rot_01"
    },
    {
      "id": "q_jee_2024_ph_02",
      "examId": "jee_main",
      "exam": "JEE Main",
      "subjectId": "physics",
      "subject": "Physics",
      "chapterId": "rotational_motion",
      "chapter": "Rotational Motion",
      "topicId": "moment_of_inertia",
      "topic": "Moment of Inertia & Rolling Motion",
      "year": 2024,
      "shift": "30 Jan Shift 2",
      "type": "NAT",
      "difficulty": "hard",
      "revisionZone": "trap_zone",
      "ncertPages": "Class 11, pp. 166-170",
      "statementHtml": "<p>A uniform disc of mass $4\\text{ kg}$ and radius $2\\text{ m}$ is rotating about an axis passing through its center and perpendicular to its plane with an angular velocity of $3\\text{ rad/s}$. Calculate its rotational kinetic energy in Joules.</p>",
      "options": [],
      "correctOption": null,
      "numericalAnswer": 36.0,
      "numericalTolerance": 0.1,
      "correctMarks": 4,
      "negativeMarks": 0,
      "solutionHtml": "<p>Moment of inertia of a uniform disc $I = \\frac{1}{2} M R^2 = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 4 \\times 2^2 = 8\\text{ kg}\\cdot\\text{m}^2$. Rotational kinetic energy $$K = \\frac{1}{2} I \\omega^2 = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 8 \\times 3^2 = 36\\text{ J}$$</p>",
      "commonMistakeType": "calculation",
      "hasVideoSolution": true,
      "videoContentId": "vdo_ph_rot_02"
    },
    {
      "id": "q_jee_2023_ph_03",
      "examId": "jee_main",
      "exam": "JEE Main",
      "subjectId": "physics",
      "subject": "Physics",
      "chapterId": "electrostatics",
      "chapter": "Electrostatics",
      "topicId": "gauss_law",
      "topic": "Electric Flux & Gauss's Law",
      "year": 2023,
      "shift": "06 Apr Shift 1",
      "type": "MCQ",
      "difficulty": "easy",
      "revisionZone": "must_revise",
      "ncertPages": "Class 12, pp. 33-40",
      "statementHtml": "<p>A charge $q$ is placed at the center of the line joining two equal charges $Q$. The system of the three charges will be in equilibrium if $q$ is equal to:</p>",
      "options": [
        { "key": "A", "html": "<p>$-\\frac{Q}{2}$</p>" },
        { "key": "B", "html": "<p>$-\\frac{Q}{4}$</p>" },
        { "key": "C", "html": "<p>$+\\frac{Q}{4}$</p>" },
        { "key": "D", "html": "<p>$+\\frac{Q}{2}$</p>" }
      ],
      "correctOption": "B",
      "numericalAnswer": null,
      "numericalTolerance": 0.0,
      "correctMarks": 4,
      "negativeMarks": -1,
      "solutionHtml": "<p>For equilibrium of extreme charge $Q$, net electrostatic force must be zero: $$\\frac{k Q^2}{(2x)^2} + \\frac{k Q q}{x^2} = 0 \\implies \\frac{Q}{4} + q = 0 \\implies q = -\\frac{Q}{4}$$</p>",
      "commonMistakeType": "conceptual",
      "hasVideoSolution": true,
      "videoContentId": "vdo_ph_elec_01"
    },
    {
      "id": "q_jee_2024_ph_04",
      "examId": "jee_main",
      "exam": "JEE Main",
      "subjectId": "physics",
      "subject": "Physics",
      "chapterId": "current_electricity",
      "chapter": "Current Electricity",
      "topicId": "kirchhoff_laws",
      "topic": "Kirchhoff's Laws & Wheatstone Bridge",
      "year": 2024,
      "shift": "01 Feb Shift 1",
      "type": "NAT",
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "revisionZone": "high_yield",
      "ncertPages": "Class 12, pp. 115-121",
      "statementHtml": "<p>In a balanced meter bridge experiment, the balancing length from the left end is found to be $40\\text{ cm}$ when a standard resistance of $12\\,\\Omega$ is placed in the right gap. Find the value of the unknown resistance in the left gap (in $\\Omega$).</p>",
      "options": [],
      "correctOption": null,
      "numericalAnswer": 8.0,
      "numericalTolerance": 0.1,
      "correctMarks": 4,
      "negativeMarks": 0,
      "solutionHtml": "<p>For a balanced meter bridge, $$\\frac{X}{R} = \\frac{l}{100 - l} \\implies \\frac{X}{12} = \\frac{40}{60} = \\frac{2}{3} \\implies X = 8\\,\\Omega$$</p>",
      "commonMistakeType": "formula_mixup",
      "hasVideoSolution": true,
      "videoContentId": "vdo_ph_curr_01"
    },
    {
      "id": "q_jee_2024_ph_05",
      "examId": "jee_advanced",
      "exam": "JEE Advanced",
      "subjectId": "physics",
      "subject": "Physics",
      "chapterId": "modern_physics",
      "chapter": "Modern Physics",
      "topicId": "photoelectric_effect",
      "topic": "Photoelectric Effect & Matter Waves",
      "year": 2024,
      "shift": "Paper 1",
      "type": "MCQ",
      "difficulty": "hard",
      "revisionZone": "high_yield",
      "ncertPages": "Class 12, pp. 388-395",
      "statementHtml": "<p>A proton and an $\\alpha$-particle are accelerated from rest through the same potential difference $V$. The ratio of their de Broglie wavelengths $\\lambda_p : \\lambda_\\alpha$ is:</p>",
      "options": [
        { "key": "A", "html": "<p>$2\\sqrt{2} : 1$</p>" },
        { "key": "B", "html": "<p>$1 : 2\\sqrt{2}$</p>" },
        { "key": "C", "html": "<p>$2 : 1$</p>" },
        { "key": "D", "html": "<p>$4 : 1$</p>" }
      ],
      "correctOption": "A",
      "numericalAnswer": null,
      "numericalTolerance": 0.0,
      "correctMarks": 4,
      "negativeMarks": -1,
      "solutionHtml": "<p>De Broglie wavelength $\\lambda = \\frac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m q V}}$. Since $m_\\alpha = 4 m_p$ and $q_\\alpha = 2 q_p$, $$\\frac{\\lambda_p}{\\lambda_\\alpha} = \\sqrt{\\frac{m_\\alpha q_\\alpha}{m_p q_p}} = \\sqrt{4 \\times 2} = 2\\sqrt{2} : 1$$</p>",
      "commonMistakeType": "silly",
      "hasVideoSolution": true,
      "videoContentId": "vdo_ph_mod_01"
    },
    {
      "id": "q_jee_2023_ph_06",
      "examId": "jee_main",
      "exam": "JEE Main",
      "subjectId": "physics",
      "subject": "Physics",
      "chapterId": "thermodynamics",
      "chapter": "Thermodynamics",
      "topicId": "carnot_engine",
      "topic": "First & Second Laws of Thermodynamics",
      "year": 2023,
      "shift": "08 Apr Shift 2",
      "type": "MCQ",
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "revisionZone": "must_revise",
      "ncertPages": "Class 11, pp. 306-315",
      "statementHtml": "<p>A Carnot engine operates between source temperature $600\\text{ K}$ and sink temperature $300\\text{ K}$. If it absorbs $1200\\text{ J}$ of heat per cycle from the source, the mechanical work done per cycle is:</p>",
      "options": [
        { "key": "A", "html": "<p>$300\\text{ J}$</p>" },
        { "key": "B", "html": "<p>$600\\text{ J}$</p>" },
        { "key": "C", "html": "<p>$800\\text{ J}$</p>" },
        { "key": "D", "html": "<p>$900\\text{ J}$</p>" }
      ],
      "correctOption": "B",
      "numericalAnswer": null,
      "numericalTolerance": 0.0,
      "correctMarks": 4,
      "negativeMarks": -1,
      "solutionHtml": "<p>Carnot efficiency $\\eta = 1 - \\frac{T_C}{T_H} = 1 - \\frac{300}{600} = 0.5$. Work done $W = \\eta Q_H = 0.5 \\times 1200 = 600\\text{ J}$.</p>",
      "commonMistakeType": "calculation",
      "hasVideoSolution": true,
      "videoContentId": "vdo_ph_thermo_01"
    },
    {
      "id": "q_jee_2024_ch_01",
      "examId": "jee_main",
      "exam": "JEE Main",
      "subjectId": "chemistry",
      "subject": "Chemistry",
      "chapterId": "chemical_kinetics",
      "chapter": "Chemical Kinetics",
      "topicId": "first_order_kinetics",
      "topic": "First Order Reactions & Half-Life",
      "year": 2024,
      "shift": "27 Jan Shift 1",
      "type": "MCQ",
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "revisionZone": "high_yield",
      "ncertPages": "Class 12, pp. 105-112",
      "statementHtml": "<p>For a first-order reaction, the time required for $99.9\\%$ completion is approximately how many times the half-life ($t_{1/2}$) of the reaction?</p>",
      "options": [
        { "key": "A", "html": "<p>$2$</p>" },
        { "key": "B", "html": "<p>$5$</p>" },
        { "key": "C", "html": "<p>$10$</p>" },
        { "key": "D", "html": "<p>$100$</p>" }
      ],
      "correctOption": "C",
      "numericalAnswer": null,
      "numericalTolerance": 0.0,
      "correctMarks": 4,
      "negativeMarks": -1,
      "solutionHtml": "<p>$$t_{99.9\\%} = \\frac{2.303}{k} \\log\\left(\\frac{100}{0.1}\\right) = \\frac{2.303 \\times 3}{k}$$ Since $t_{1/2} = \\frac{2.303 \\times 0.3010}{k}$, $\\frac{t_{99.9\\%}}{t_{1/2}} = \\frac{3}{0.3010} \\approx 10$.</p>",
      "commonMistakeType": "calculation",
      "hasVideoSolution": true,
      "videoContentId": "vdo_ch_kin_01"
    },
    {
      "id": "q_jee_2024_ch_02",
      "examId": "jee_main",
      "exam": "JEE Main",
      "subjectId": "chemistry",
      "subject": "Chemistry",
      "chapterId": "electrochemistry",
      "chapter": "Electrochemistry",
      "topicId": "nernst_equation",
      "topic": "Nernst Equation & Gibbs Free Energy",
      "year": 2024,
      "shift": "29 Jan Shift 2",
      "type": "MCQ",
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "revisionZone": "high_yield",
      "ncertPages": "Class 12, pp. 68-74",
      "statementHtml": "<p>For the Daniell cell $\\text{Zn}(s) + \\text{Cu}^{2+}(aq) \\rightarrow \\text{Zn}^{2+}(aq) + \\text{Cu}(s)$, if the standard cell potential $E^\\circ_{\\text{cell}} = 1.10\\text{ V}$, what is the standard Gibbs free energy change $\\Delta G^\\circ$ (given $F = 96500\\text{ C/mol}$)?</p>",
      "options": [
        { "key": "A", "html": "<p>$-212.3\\text{ kJ/mol}$</p>" },
        { "key": "B", "html": "<p>$-106.15\\text{ kJ/mol}$</p>" },
        { "key": "C", "html": "<p>$+212.3\\text{ kJ/mol}$</p>" },
        { "key": "D", "html": "<p>$-424.6\\text{ kJ/mol}$</p>" }
      ],
      "correctOption": "A",
      "numericalAnswer": null,
      "numericalTolerance": 0.0,
      "correctMarks": 4,
      "negativeMarks": -1,
      "solutionHtml": "<p>Here $n = 2$ moles of electrons transferred. $$\\Delta G^\\circ = -n F E^\\circ_{\\text{cell}} = -2 \\times 96500 \\times 1.10 = -212300\\text{ J/mol} = -212.3\\text{ kJ/mol}$$</p>",
      "commonMistakeType": "formula_mixup",
      "hasVideoSolution": true,
      "videoContentId": "vdo_ch_elec_01"
    },
    {
