    {
      "id": "q_jee_2024_ch_04",
      "examId": "jee_advanced",
      "exam": "JEE Advanced",
      "subjectId": "chemistry",
      "subject": "Chemistry",
      "chapterId": "thermodynamics_chem",
      "chapter": "Chemical Thermodynamics",
      "topicId": "gibbs_spontaneity",
      "topic": "Entropy & Gibbs Energy",
      "year": 2024,
      "shift": "Paper 2",
      "type": "MCQ",
      "difficulty": "hard",
      "revisionZone": "must_revise",
      "ncertPages": "Class 11, pp. 176-184",
      "statementHtml": "<p>For a chemical reaction with $\\Delta H^\\circ = +40\\text{ kJ/mol}$ and $\\Delta S^\\circ = +100\\text{ J}\\cdot\\text{K}^{-1}\\text{mol}^{-1}$, the reaction becomes spontaneous above the temperature:</p>",
      "options": [
        { "key": "A", "html": "<p>$250\\text{ K}$</p>" },
        { "key": "B", "html": "<p>$400\\text{ K}$</p>" },
        { "key": "C", "html": "<p>$300\\text{ K}$</p>" },
        { "key": "D", "html": "<p>$500\\text{ K}$</p>" }
      ],
      "correctOption": "B",
      "numericalAnswer": null,
      "numericalTolerance": 0.0,
      "correctMarks": 4,
      "negativeMarks": -1,
      "solutionHtml": "<p>For spontaneity, $\\Delta G = \\Delta H - T \\Delta S < 0 \\implies T > \\frac{\\Delta H}{\\Delta S} = \\frac{40000\\text{ J/mol}}{100\\text{ J}\\cdot\\text{K}^{-1}\\text{mol}^{-1}} = 400\\text{ K}$.</p>",
      "commonMistakeType": "silly",
      "hasVideoSolution": true,
      "videoContentId": "vdo_ch_thermo_01"
    },
    {
      "id": "q_jee_2024_ma_01",
